    "case_id": "573",
    "case_summary": "A 61-year-old man with a medical history of factor V Leiden mutation (discovered after an episode of abdominal venous thromboembolism), hypothyroidism, uncontrolled type 2 diabetes mellitus, and four transient ischemic attacks. The patient presented with memory impairment and behavioral changes - he could not state the reason for his office visit, had difficulty finding his way back to his car, and would forget why he went to grocery stores or how to return home. He had received anticoagulation with enoxaparin for a year previously. No recent medication changes were reported.\n\n- Blood pressure: 160/100 mm Hg- Mental status: Oriented to place and person- Neurological: Decreased sensation to touch and pinprick on bilateral upper and lower extremities and face\n\nLaboratory Tests:- Blood glucose: 21.53 mmol/L (388 mg/dL)- Leukocyte count: 11.5 × 10⁹/L with basophilia of 2.7%- Thyroid-stimulating hormone: 2.340 μU/mL- CSF glucose: 8.77 mmol/L (158 mg/dL)- CSF protein: 370 mg/L- Bone marrow flow cytometry: 60% blastsImaging Studies:- CT head: No acute intracranial pathology- MRI brain and cervical/thoracic/lumbar spine: Unremarkable- CT chest/abdomen/pelvis: UnremarkablePathological Studies:- Bone marrow biopsy: Hypercellular marrow packed with immature cells- Immunostaining: Positive for CD34, CD10, CD19; negative for CD20 and CD33- Chromosomal analysis: Philadelphia chromosome t(9;22)(q34;q11.2)- Heme malignancy fusion panel: BCR/ABL1 fusion with p210 oncoproteinImages:- Figure 1: Bone marrow biopsy (H&E stain, 20×) showing hypercellular marrow packed with blasts- Figure 2: CD34 immunostaining highlighting bone marrow packed with blasts- Figure 3: CD10 immunostaining highlighting lymphoid blasts",
